WEBVTT

00:00:09,580 --> 00:00:17,080
How are you making fruit jams that aren't full of sugar? Do you have a good sugar-free or low sugar recipe?

00:00:18,940 --> 00:00:28,940
You can if you get the shurjale, which I do use shurjale, I know there's recipes to make jams without the pectin,

00:00:29,340 --> 00:00:35,220
but I use pectin in my fruit jams that I make. You can make the low sugar

00:00:35,780 --> 00:00:44,500
portion in the shurjale recipe. They do have a recipe for that. Personally, I don't do the low sugar.

00:00:45,060 --> 00:00:50,580
You just don't eat a lot of jams. I mean, you're only putting a tablespoon of jams on your

00:00:51,380 --> 00:00:55,620
bread or whatever you use it. Of course, you're not like you're eating the whole jar full of jams,

00:00:55,620 --> 00:01:02,660
so I'm not that concerned about that. I love making jams. That's one of my favorite things to

00:01:02,660 --> 00:01:12,580
make. I make blueberry jam, raspberry jam, strawberry jam. This summer I found wild grapes at our

00:01:12,580 --> 00:01:19,300
office, which was a really fun find. I'd used them. We had a gallon of grape juice, and I thought,

00:01:19,300 --> 00:01:23,780
well, we don't have grape juice. I thought, I'm going to make jam out of it. So I made grape jelly,

00:01:23,780 --> 00:01:30,020
out of it. It was really fun. So it's first time I ever made grape jelly. We give our jamb away,

00:01:30,020 --> 00:01:38,580
our kids and our grandkids are who eat most of our jams. So something I just like making. I like

00:01:38,580 --> 00:01:44,820
doing it. It's something I really enjoy doing is kidding. So I know it's weird for a guy to like

00:01:44,820 --> 00:01:50,100
to do that, but I really like to do kidding. Hot water bath and pressure kidding, pressure can

00:01:50,100 --> 00:01:58,340
a lot of meat. When we lived it, we used to live up in the woods, 280 acres, and we were off grid.

00:01:58,340 --> 00:02:05,460
So we didn't have really refrigeration options. We had a fairly small propane refrigerator with a

00:02:05,460 --> 00:02:12,580
little TDW freezer. So freezing meat just wasn't an option. We only had a few solar panels. So

00:02:13,140 --> 00:02:21,140
then we had propane lights. So we used mainly caracene lamps in our house. So we didn't have

00:02:21,140 --> 00:02:27,860
refrigeration. So we had to learn how to can. So when we got a deer, which I mean, you could

00:02:27,860 --> 00:02:35,700
literally just step off our deck and chew the deer literally. And we would, we'd butcher it up

00:02:35,700 --> 00:02:44,340
and can it. So I found that canned meat is the best. It's just absolutely the best because it's just

00:02:44,340 --> 00:02:50,340
tender. You could even take a pork cut and meat and pressure can it. And you just have the best,

00:02:50,340 --> 00:02:57,060
you know, beef stroganoff or medicine stroganoff in the world. So we got into doing that

00:02:57,060 --> 00:03:04,740
homestead stuff back before it was cool. In this generation, there's a lot of people doing it,

00:03:04,740 --> 00:03:12,020
which is really, really exciting. But we've done that, you know, for back in the 90s when we lived

00:03:12,020 --> 00:03:12,740
up in the woods.

